1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:10,180
 We've already seen Indians of North America.

2
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:12,000
 Now suppose we have a look at what happened

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,100
 some 1,500 kilometers south of the present day Mexican border.

4
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,600
 The sombreros around Mexico City, eh?

5
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:29,120
 Well, the year is 1486.

6
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,980
 We're in Aztec country.

7
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:34,780
 Now, they have the same monuments, the same pyramids,

8
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:37,020
 same lifestyle as their cousins farther north.

9
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:40,120
 But their social order, their society, that's different.

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:51,520
 Now, here we are probably some 20,000 years after the first tribes crossed the Bering Straits from Siberia, and, well, they ended up here.

11
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:06,660
 They'll have some good things, eh?

12
00:01:06,700 --> 00:01:09,060
 Yeah, they'll have skins, cloth feathers, not to mention jewels.

13
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:10,400
 So what do we do? What do we do? What do we do?

14
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:12,660
 Now, don't get excited. Now, give them time to get here.

15
00:01:13,780 --> 00:01:15,720
 Calm down. Don't move. I'll give you a signal.

16
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:49,080
 This is some profession.

17
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:59,030
 Some profession.

18
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:28,030
 Great honor for us to receive dignitary from Mexico City. How about a little pulque to honor visit?

19
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:31,450
 Uh, we didn't come to drink. We're on business, and we don't drink and drive.

20
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:36,350
 Since you put it that way, no pulque can we do for you.

21
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:39,570
 Our merchants are endlessly attacked and robbed near your city.

22
00:02:40,170 --> 00:02:40,450
 What?

23
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:43,450
 We don't like it. We don't like it at all.

24
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:47,300
 Therefore, you will hereafter be under the protection of our grand and venerated emperor,

25
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:49,260
 and you will submit to all our laws.

26
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:52,870
 Yes, all first. Just what are these laws of your...

27
00:02:52,870 --> 00:02:53,890
 No, nothing too harsh.

28
00:02:54,190 --> 00:02:56,910
 For a start, you send to Mexico City a splendid gift in gold,

29
00:02:56,910 --> 00:03:00,140
 but, like an effort, a nice gift in jewels, feathers, coats...

30
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:00,520
 What?

31
00:03:00,860 --> 00:03:02,140
 Well, you know the sort of thing.

32
00:03:02,740 --> 00:03:07,520
 In addition, the prize spot in your temple is reserved exclusively for our sun god, Vici de Polchi.

33
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:10,670
 At least you know what you want. And was there anything else?

34
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:15,630
 You might throw in one or two magnificent young slaves, boys and girls. I'd say that about covers it.

35
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:17,880
 I see. Give me time to think it over.

36
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:20,120
 You do that, but think it over fast.

37
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:24,460
 Meanwhile, I bought you some trinkets, just in case you need them.

38
00:03:26,060 --> 00:03:27,750
 Shields and spears.

39
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:33,130
 At Tenochtitlan, Mexico, the superb Aztec capital.

40
00:03:33,290 --> 00:03:40,930
 One 20-day month has gone by, and their chief still hasn't given in.

41
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,600
 Now it's time to go and warn the Texcocos.

42
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:48,540
 And I suppose you won't drink our pulque either.

43
00:03:48,820 --> 00:03:50,620
 I suppose, well, what is it? Why'd you come?

44
00:03:50,850 --> 00:03:54,230
 Our friends in Mexico City have been waiting for a month, and they're angry.

45
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:57,230
 We're allies with the Texcoco. We can still arrange things.

46
00:03:57,570 --> 00:04:02,650
 Otherwise, in strict conformity with the law, we will have to break your neck, and it'd be a shame.

47
00:04:06,110 --> 00:04:08,090
 Gifts, nice shields, nice spears, hmm?

48
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:10,330
 To arm your warriors, hmm?

49
00:04:11,210 --> 00:04:15,510
 And also, a small quantity of our sacred oil.

50
00:04:17,150 --> 00:04:19,790
 With this, you can resist any attack by any army.

51
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:24,430
 Since you're a noble and courageous man, chief,

52
00:04:25,010 --> 00:04:27,510
 I'll give you another 20 days to give in.

53
00:04:31,220 --> 00:04:35,900
 20 suns and moons, and still there's no answer.

54
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:37,560
 The Clacopans have to be warned.

55
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:41,340
 Your wise elders wouldn't listen to the Mexico men or the Texcocos.

56
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:43,140
 That's why we, the allies of the Clacopans,

57
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:45,100
 decided to speak directly with you, warriors,

58
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,420
 who have to come out now and help our troops.

59
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:49,920
 Now, you will agree to this, or we'll devastate your villages

60
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,420
 and take all of you as slaves.

61
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:55,110
 The same, we brought you gifts.

62
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:59,980
 You have another 20 days to think it over, but this is your last chance.

63
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:01,820
 After that, it'll be too late.

64
00:05:29,950 --> 00:05:34,020
 We're right into the trap set for the Mexicans.

65
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,100
 Confirmed victory for the Mexico men.

66
00:06:49,710 --> 00:07:41,280
 In the city, and there are over us.

67
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:48,490
 Beautiful. Is that where they're going to sacrifice us?

68
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:49,310
 Yes, I'm afraid so.

69
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:52,170
 Oh, that will be a great honor for us, venerated father.

70
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,410
 Father's in school.

71
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:04,870
 A great priest here among us today.

72
00:08:04,870 --> 00:08:08,290
 Listen with great respect.

73
00:08:11,250 --> 00:08:11,970
 You are my children.

74
00:08:12,590 --> 00:08:16,330
 Today I will speak of our great god, warrior of the sun, Huitzilopochtli.

75
00:08:16,450 --> 00:08:19,750
 Our world is coming to an end, for the sun has nearly completed its course.

76
00:08:20,290 --> 00:08:23,730
 The sun must receive new energy every day if it is to reappear.

77
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:27,280
 This source of energy, it is precious water.

78
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:29,140
 That is human blood.

79
00:08:31,460 --> 00:08:35,460
 That is why we must take prisoners to satisfy our sun god.

80
00:08:45,390 --> 00:08:59,140
 The sun comes back at dawn only if we make sacrifices, so we must take prisoners.

81
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:03,340
 Yes, and to take prisoners, we need warriors.

82
00:09:04,090 --> 00:09:05,670
 To be a warrior, what a calling.

83
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:08,970
 Yes, I recall only too well when I was a young warrior.

84
00:09:09,830 --> 00:09:10,870
 Yes, yes.

85
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:30,890
 In my path, give you a strong body and an iron will,

86
00:09:31,070 --> 00:09:33,670
 forge a sterling character and a spirit of public service.

87
00:09:33,870 --> 00:09:35,830
 All this is the purpose of your education.

88
00:09:36,270 --> 00:09:39,650
 And you girls, you may aspire to the coveted title of priestess,

89
00:09:39,690 --> 00:09:41,550
 or you can marry if you so desire.

90
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:45,870
 But you boys, at the age of ten, your hair is cut,

91
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:46,950
 leaving only a hank.

92
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:49,990
 That bit will be cut.

93
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:53,510
 The day you bring in your first live prisoner,

94
00:09:55,310 --> 00:10:00,820
 you can even group into twos and threes.

95
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:02,400
 Yeah, no, that's allowed.

96
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:18,210
 Three battles, and if you haven't brought in any prisoners,

97
00:10:18,390 --> 00:10:19,310
 then you are demoted.

98
00:10:19,910 --> 00:10:22,390
 Yes, and you have to return to civilian life.

99
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:27,040
 On the other hand, suppose you take or kill four enemy warriors,

100
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:28,600
 then you're promoted to superior grade,

101
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:30,940
 and you're chiefs, knights of the jaguar, knights of the eagle,

102
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:32,200
 honored by the emperor?

103
00:10:33,390 --> 00:10:36,690
 How will our honored and most gallant warriors come?

104
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:54,660
 Who dies in combat?

105
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:58,440
 Or, upon the sacrificial stone, becomes a companion of the sun

106
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:01,320
 and takes his place in the brilliant aura around that star.

107
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:05,830
 For life and death are just two forms of the same reality.

108
00:11:06,810 --> 00:11:09,610
 Our warriors guarantee our liberty.

109
00:11:09,770 --> 00:11:12,990
 It is their valor and courage that have built our empire, but you know that.

110
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,210
 You don't really know all that much about it.

111
00:11:16,450 --> 00:11:17,880
 Could you tell us some more about it?

112
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:20,320
 Yes, tell us. Tell us the old...

113
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:21,900
 Since you ask.

114
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:26,100
 Yes, it was some 15,000 years ago our ancestors hunted mammoths.

115
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:29,560
 Yes, but then they settled down and started planting crops.

116
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:33,040
 And they created ceramic objects, built temples, tumulus for their gods.

117
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,040
 They were very talented, wouldn't you say?

118
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:38,460
 They invented a talent, writing, arts of all kinds.

119
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:42,390
 And then the Mexicas, a small tribe, they would hunt.

120
00:11:42,830 --> 00:11:44,330
 And they'd keep on the move.

121
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:52,050
 They had a very great priest, an ancient relative of mine, and a great god, Huitzilopochtli.

122
00:11:52,990 --> 00:11:55,680
 And one day...

123
00:11:55,680 --> 00:11:59,440
 Soon you'll see eagle perched on cactus, devouring snake.

124
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:02,360
 There you will build my temple and city for the people.

125
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:05,480
 I don't think I quite understand it.

126
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:06,940
 He's a snake devouring a cactus.

127
00:12:07,160 --> 00:12:08,920
 There's an eagle sitting on a snake.

128
00:12:10,570 --> 00:12:12,910
 Messages from gods can be very inscrutable.

129
00:12:21,160 --> 00:12:26,050
 Over there, do you see what I see?

130
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:30,250
 An eagle on a cactus, and he's devouring a snake.

131
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:31,970
 It's here.

132
00:12:32,290 --> 00:12:34,770
 On this spot, we will construct our own city.

133
00:12:35,230 --> 00:12:36,510
 We're here and nowhere else.

134
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:38,050
 The gods have so told us.

135
00:12:38,190 --> 00:12:39,330
 Now all of you get to work.

136
00:12:39,410 --> 00:12:39,650
 Work!

137
00:12:39,890 --> 00:12:44,800
 Otherwise, the sky will fall on our heads and the sun will stand still in the sky.

138
00:12:47,850 --> 00:12:55,430
 Of course, in time, there were wars and treachery, murders, an ordinary run of history.

139
00:12:56,460 --> 00:13:03,970
 But a wise prince, Itzhakotl, created a confederation with the people of Texcoco and Tlacopan.

140
00:13:03,970 --> 00:13:11,350
 Today, our city is the most beautiful in the whole world, and tomorrow, tomorrow, we will consecrate our great temple.

141
00:13:51,070 --> 00:14:09,530
 Priestess had enough for today.

142
00:14:10,670 --> 00:14:12,030
 That's enough.

143
00:14:12,250 --> 00:14:13,650
 The gods have had their share.

144
00:14:15,010 --> 00:14:16,960
 You were a brave chief in war.

145
00:14:17,430 --> 00:14:18,600
 Would you be free?

146
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,600
 We will give you an army to command, and we'll all respect you.

147
00:14:22,310 --> 00:14:24,910
 Why do you insult your son, venerated father?

148
00:14:24,910 --> 00:14:26,610
 Have I not earned an honorable death?

149
00:14:26,610 --> 00:14:28,910
 Go on, tell your priest to make one last effort.

150
00:14:30,950 --> 00:14:32,860
 What do you say we fly a little?

151
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:34,570
 Right, that is a very good idea.

152
00:14:45,670 --> 00:14:48,800
 Who else here would like to go flying with us?

153
00:14:49,290 --> 00:14:49,890
 Me!

154
00:15:01,260 --> 00:15:23,840
 It was cream in a thing.

155
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:25,730
 A little too much pull came.

156
00:15:26,120 --> 00:15:26,620
 Not at all.

157
00:15:26,620 --> 00:15:29,720
 No, I assure you, officer, we just went for a little spin.

158
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:30,620
 The word of honor.

159
00:15:33,830 --> 00:15:34,850
 Smell your breath.

160
00:15:37,330 --> 00:15:45,200
 Not bad. No trace of alcohol. This time you were lucky. As you know, drunken driving here is punishable by death.

161
00:15:45,590 --> 00:15:47,250
 You've been warned. Good day.

162
00:15:48,190 --> 00:15:54,230
 What that fellow should use is a breathalyzer. It's a real shame it hasn't been invented yet.

163
00:15:54,230 --> 00:15:58,660
 Now tell you some of the laws and regulations that we're all expected to obey.

164
00:15:59,410 --> 00:16:02,410
 Now that it's come up, why not start with laws on drunkenness?

165
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:08,600
 It's not allowed, except for very old people. It's like a destructive whirlpool.

166
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:12,420
 It would destroy all that is good in our city.

167
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:15,160
 All manner of evil doing springs from it.

168
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:20,800
 Crimes and all sorts of traffic accidents and misdemeanors.

169
00:16:21,090 --> 00:16:24,460
 That's why any man caught in a drunken state.

170
00:16:25,020 --> 00:16:28,500
 If he's a commoner, he's roundly beaten and his head is shaved.

171
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:31,380
 If a nobleman, he's hanged in public.

172
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:39,500
 Fate awaits anyone who's caught using peyotl.

173
00:16:40,020 --> 00:16:42,980
 There are other drugs that make you see and hear things that aren't there.

174
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:45,360
 Another sort of drunkenness, but just as harmful.

175
00:16:45,360 --> 00:16:53,530
 Stealing? If committed by an ordinary commoner, here be made a slave for a period of time.

176
00:16:53,530 --> 00:16:58,300
 If committed by a nobleman, it means death.

177
00:16:58,300 --> 00:17:06,050
 Cheating? Or changing the boundaries of your property to the detriment of others, death.

178
00:17:06,050 --> 00:17:16,930
 Crime of adultery. The two guilty parties will have their heads crushed.

179
00:17:16,930 --> 00:17:25,500
 Homosexuality? It depends on the sex of the couple. For women, it is death. For men, well, nobody's perfect.

180
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:29,080
 Oh, yes. A few pertinent remarks concerning slavery.

181
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:34,430
 The slave will be respected and cannot be resold unless three masters complain about him.

182
00:17:34,430 --> 00:17:40,250
 And if he escapes and manages to reach the emperor's palace, he is free.

183
00:17:40,250 --> 00:17:43,240
 Oh, yes. I nearly forgot a rather important detail.

184
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:48,960
 Anyone committing a crime has the right to confess once and that one time he or she will not be punished for that crime.

185
00:17:48,960 --> 00:18:11,070
 It's almost half of our corn harvest he's claiming is a legal tax.

186
00:18:11,070 --> 00:18:13,020
 Sure, but, oh, that's nothing, friend.

187
00:18:13,020 --> 00:18:17,600
 You're forgotten? During the harvest, we had to take time off so we could work on the dikes.

188
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:23,580
 Yeah, to build the temple of Zoccipoli, the god of summer. But no, that's nothing.

189
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:26,370
 And all the hours we spent on the second aqueduct.

190
00:18:26,510 --> 00:18:30,490
 To have more turkeys to give to the emperor. But that's nothing, friend.

191
00:18:30,650 --> 00:18:32,170
 What do you mean, nothing? You're kidding.

192
00:18:32,330 --> 00:18:34,910
 Sure, don't you remember what happened to me two years ago?

193
00:18:35,310 --> 00:18:40,740
 I harvested 40 baskets of corn, and the tax collector said I had to give him 20 of them.

194
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:43,050
 Well, 20 basketfuls.

195
00:18:43,050 --> 00:18:45,370
 Well, I have to save four of them for seed corn.

196
00:18:47,290 --> 00:18:48,790
 For my next crop.

197
00:18:49,040 --> 00:18:50,630
 And what do you think he said, Dan?

198
00:18:50,970 --> 00:18:51,770
 Hold on.

199
00:18:52,130 --> 00:18:52,740
 I was forgetting.

200
00:18:53,020 --> 00:18:54,760
 There's an increase in taxation this year.

201
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:55,820
 You owe me a dozen more.

202
00:18:55,980 --> 00:18:58,640
 How do you expect me to feed my family, all my children?

203
00:18:58,860 --> 00:19:00,920
 I'll hardly have enough corn for tortillas.

204
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:01,780
 And he says...

205
00:19:01,780 --> 00:19:03,170
 Use red beans and black.

206
00:19:03,460 --> 00:19:04,320
 In tortillas?

207
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:05,400
 Yeah.

208
00:19:06,700 --> 00:19:09,350
 Do you happen to have red beans?

209
00:19:09,610 --> 00:19:11,990
 Well, sure, I have about seven or eight sacks back there.

210
00:19:12,430 --> 00:19:13,990
 You know what he had the nerve to say?

211
00:19:14,270 --> 00:19:15,610
 No, at this point, I couldn't guess.

212
00:19:15,690 --> 00:19:17,710
 He just smiled and said...

213
00:19:17,710 --> 00:19:19,670
 It's luck that you mentioned it.

214
00:19:19,870 --> 00:19:20,970
 Tax on red beans, too.

215
00:19:21,050 --> 00:19:22,210
 That'll be four socks.

216
00:19:22,370 --> 00:19:23,330
 So I had no choice.

217
00:19:23,410 --> 00:19:25,830
 I gave him those two, but he still wasn't satisfied.

218
00:19:26,300 --> 00:19:27,220
 Know what he did then?

219
00:19:27,570 --> 00:19:28,760
 No, the mind boggles.

220
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:29,180
 Go on.

221
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:30,940
 He asked me if I had any black ants.

222
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:32,900
 Honest, he asked me if I had black ants.

223
00:19:35,090 --> 00:19:35,730
 Incredible, then.

224
00:19:35,730 --> 00:19:36,550
 So what did you say?

225
00:19:36,860 --> 00:19:39,540
 If you want to know, we're infested with black ants.

226
00:19:39,920 --> 00:19:42,240
 And they're causing me all kinds of trouble.

227
00:19:42,830 --> 00:19:43,790
 Be generous.

228
00:19:43,910 --> 00:19:44,650
 You keep your ants.

229
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:47,620
 Then he packed himself up and was off like lightning.

230
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:49,480
 Disappeared in no time at all.

231
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:50,460
 Could have been worse.

232
00:19:50,680 --> 00:19:51,460
 Oh, worse.

233
00:19:51,620 --> 00:19:52,500
 Could have happened to me.

234
00:19:53,950 --> 00:19:57,290
 It can't be much further. We'll be there soon.

235
00:19:57,290 --> 00:20:01,660
 Yes, you're right. Once we're there, we'll have to bring the cloth we have to give in exchange.

236
00:20:01,660 --> 00:20:07,930
 Not merchants.

237
00:20:07,930 --> 00:20:10,170
 Okay, Chief, but they could be rich peasants.

238
00:20:10,170 --> 00:20:14,200
 And a good harvest, Chief. We don't mind grabbing that, Chief.

239
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:18,400
 We don't mind at all. It takes a lot less work than growing the stuff in the hot sun.

240
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:45,010
 At my signal.

241
00:20:45,010 --> 00:21:45,900
 And General Chief, his head's over there.

242
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:54,600
 What?

243
00:21:54,600 --> 00:22:02,620
 Who's your future daughter-in-law?

244
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:32,630
 Parents arrange things with the marriage brokers.

245
00:22:32,630 --> 00:22:35,470
 You see, our son is strong and brave.

246
00:22:35,470 --> 00:22:37,660
 Yes, so he appears. Quite a good catch.

247
00:22:37,660 --> 00:23:23,980
 In any case, she's still too young to think.

248
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:49,920
 No return tomorrow, and we'll discuss it again.

249
00:23:49,920 --> 00:24:16,570
 She's ugly, good for nothing, but if you insist, we'll consider the question.

250
00:24:16,570 --> 00:24:21,380
 People, I'm here to ask for your daughter's hand in marriage, you agree?

251
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:23,660
 Sorry, lad, but you've come just a bit too late.

252
00:24:23,660 --> 00:24:29,860
 What? That's not fair. I mean, well, it's not fair.

253
00:24:29,860 --> 00:24:51,740
 Except I'm married. Hooray!

254
00:24:51,740 --> 00:24:59,830
 Drink so much, you know what they say. Drink as a knave, end up a slave.

255
00:25:04,050 --> 00:25:09,700
 You drunk and disorderly on a public street, you've had it.

256
00:25:09,700 --> 00:25:12,900
 Talk about a code of good conduct, meh.

257
00:25:13,220 --> 00:25:18,040
 Now, when you eat, never use all your fingers, but only the three fingers of the right hand.

258
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:21,160
 When you eat and drink, you must do so silently.

259
00:25:21,380 --> 00:25:23,640
 After all, you're not so many puppies.

260
00:25:24,370 --> 00:25:27,750
 Anyway, be careful not to drop food on your clothes and stain them.

261
00:25:28,100 --> 00:25:32,100
 If you see or hear something distasteful, keep still about it and be discreet.

262
00:25:32,100 --> 00:25:38,980
 Do not leap upon and grab women as if an animal at feeding time.

263
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:45,500
 In your attire, be neither too precise nor outlandish.

264
00:25:45,640 --> 00:25:48,280
 Above all, never wear dirty or torn clothing.

265
00:25:49,900 --> 00:25:52,350
 Now I must leave you, my children.

266
00:25:52,610 --> 00:25:53,830
 Other duties call me.

267
00:25:54,480 --> 00:25:56,500
 I'm truly sorry, for I like you all.

268
00:25:56,560 --> 00:25:57,960
 Any questions you want to ask?

269
00:25:58,890 --> 00:26:02,490
 Sir, the other day I was at the great feast in the temple.

270
00:26:02,590 --> 00:26:04,610
 I'm ashamed, but there was blood everywhere there.

271
00:26:04,910 --> 00:26:06,170
 I didn't like seeing it.

272
00:26:06,510 --> 00:26:10,440
 Tell me, wise master, do the gods always thirst for the blood of men?

273
00:26:10,770 --> 00:26:14,200
 I understand how you must have felt, but not all gods are like that.

274
00:26:14,200 --> 00:26:16,700
 In the past, we had a god who was fair and good,

275
00:26:16,820 --> 00:26:19,080
 Quetzalcoatl, the plumed serpent god.

276
00:26:19,460 --> 00:26:21,500
 He was the enemy of all violence.

277
00:26:21,760 --> 00:26:26,100
 In sacrifices, he took only the blood of serpents and birds, hence his name.

278
00:26:26,100 --> 00:26:31,130
 But Tezcatlipoca, who is a wicked god, a magician, jealous of his goodness,

279
00:26:31,610 --> 00:26:33,850
 bewitched him and chased him away.

280
00:26:37,780 --> 00:26:43,040
 And our divine Quetzalcoatl went to exile across the water of the eastern seas.

281
00:26:43,160 --> 00:26:47,240
 But one day soon, he will return. Believe it, all the oracles tell us this.

282
00:26:47,610 --> 00:26:50,720
 And 30 years later, from across that eastern sea,

283
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:54,340
 Taken for the god Quetzalcoatl came Cortes, the conquistador.

284
00:26:54,660 --> 00:26:58,340
 And it was the end of the Aztec dynasty, cities, palaces, pyramids.

285
00:26:58,750 --> 00:27:02,810
 All this splendor was to disappear, swept away by a horrible nightmare.

